{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 40, :comments ["<b>And the sojourning of the children of Israel, who dwelled in Egypt, thirty years and four hundred years</b> — From the birth of Isaac until now was four hundred years, for from the time that Abraham had seed, the verse \"for your seed shall be a sojourner in a land that is not theirs\" (Genesis 15:13) was fulfilled in him; and thirty years had elapsed from the time the decree between the pieces was issued until Isaac was born. When you count four hundred years from the birth of Isaac, you will find from their entry into Egypt until their departure to be two hundred and ten years. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Exodus 12:40), and it is the opinion of our Sages (in the Mekhilta here). But it is not fully correct in its explanation, for it is written (Genesis 12:4), \"And Abram was seventy-five years old when he departed from Haran,\" and the covenant between the pieces took place many days after that.\n\nRather, we must resolve the matter as we learned in Seder Olam (chapter 1): our father Abraham was seventy years old when He spoke with him between the pieces, as it is said (Exodus 12:41), \"And it came to pass at the end of thirty years and four hundred years, all the hosts of YHWH went out from the land of Egypt\"; he then returned to Haran and spent five years there, as it is said (Genesis 12:4), \"And Abram was seventy-five years old when he departed from Haran.\" The meaning of the verse is thus to say that when he departed from Haran, the land of his birth, from which he never again returned to see his father's house, he was seventy-five years old.\n\nMy opinion by way of the plain sense is that YHWH said to Abraham: \"Know well that before I give you this land, your seed shall be a sojourner in a land that is not theirs for many days, four hundred years\" — and He did not bother to inform him of the thirty, because He further said to him, \"and in the fourth generation they shall return here\" (Genesis 15:16), to inform him that they would not return immediately at the end of four hundred, but only in the fourth generation, when the iniquity of the Amorite would be complete. He thereby alluded to these thirty years, for they spent forty years in the wilderness not because of the iniquity of the Amorite, which had not yet been completed.\n\nThe reading of the verse would thus be: <b>And the sojourning of the children of Israel, who dwelled in Egypt</b> — until <b>thirty years and four hundred years</b> that they dwelled there — to complete for them the full term in a land not theirs, Scripture informs us that now, upon their departure, the exile decreed upon them was fulfilled, and He brought them out from servitude to freedom — not that He would bring them out of Egypt and they would still be sojourners in a land not theirs. And since Scripture had already mentioned this matter and made it known, there was no need to elaborate here, for this verse came only to inform us of the thirty years that were added to them. Therefore it stated briefly that they completed in Egypt the four hundred years that had been told to Abraham their father and were known to them, plus another thirty years; and then it repeated: <b>And it came to pass at the end of thirty years and four hundred years</b> of their exile, <b>they went out from the land of Egypt</b> to everlasting freedom.\n\nA parallel to this is (Deuteronomy 2:14), \"And the days in which we walked from Kadesh-barnea until we crossed the Wadi Zered were thirty-eight years\" — to complete the reckoning, for they did not walk from Kadesh-barnea to the Wadi Zered for thirty-eight years; rather, they stayed in Kadesh many years, and afterward they walked from there and turned back toward the way of the Sea of Reeds, and in the thirty-eighth year they crossed the Wadi Zered. The reading of the verse is thus: \"And the days in which we walked from Kadesh-barnea until we crossed the Wadi Zered — until thirty-eight years.\" And similarly, \"Happy is the one who waits and reaches one thousand three hundred and thirty-five days\" (Daniel 12:12) — who reaches their appointed end, not the days themselves."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {0 "Ramban quotes Exodus 12:41 with the reading שְׁלֹשִׁים שָׁנָה וְאַרְבַּע מֵאוֹת שָׁנָה (thirty years and four hundred years) rather than the standard Masoretic אַרְבַּע מֵאוֹת וּשְׁלֹשִׁים שָׁנָה; the translation preserves his reading faithfully since the argument depends on it."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:07:12.006956965Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}